    "title": "Iron Deficiency Anemia: Symptoms, Causes, and Treatment",
    "visibility": "Public",
    "content": "<h2>Common Symptoms of Iron Deficiency Anemia</h2>\n<p>Iron deficiency anemia can take time to occur. However, as iron decreases in the body, a person might experience feelings of fatigue and weakness and find it hard to perform their everyday tasks.</p>\n<ul>\n\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Causes & Risk Factors</h2>\n<p>Iron-deficiency anemia occurs if the body lacks sufficient amounts of iron, if it fails to absorb iron effectively, or if there is too much bleeding for the body to compensate. Menstrual problems, pregnancy, digestive disorders, and blood loss all put one at increased risk of iron-deficiency anemia.</p>\n\n<h2>How Iron Deficiency Anemia Is Treated</h2>\n<p>It will depend on the reason that the iron levels are low. Iron supplementation will be necessary for some individuals, whereas some individuals might need treatment for another condition like bleeding or reduced iron absorption. Dietary modifications will also assist in increasing iron levels. Iron supplements must be taken based on professional recommendation since excess iron will be dangerous.</p>\n",
    "imageCaption": "Iron Deficiency Anemia",
    "quickAnswer": "Iron Deficiency Anemia is the lack of adequate amounts of iron in the body needed to manufacture enough hemoglobin, which transports oxygen in the blood. Symptoms include fatigue, weakness, dizziness, headaches, and difficulty breathing. The approach to management involves the identification of the underlying cause and repletion of iron levels by either diet or supplements.",
    "allowComments": true,
