    "title": "Designing Your Weekly Workout Routine: A Clinical Approach to Fitness",
    "faqs": [
      {
        "id": "faq-wk-1",
        "question": "Is it better to exercise in the morning or evening?",
        "answer": "The time of day has a modest effect on performance for some people, but consistency matters far more than timing. The best time to exercise is the time you will actually do it regularly. Some people find morning exercise easier to fit in before the day's demands build up; others find evening sessions work better. Vigorous exercise very close to bedtime may interfere with sleep for some individuals, but this varies."
      },
      {
        "id": "faq-wk-2",
        "question": "How long before I see results from a new exercise routine?",
        "answer": "Cardiovascular fitness can begin to improve within two to four weeks of consistent training. Strength gains become noticeable within four to six weeks, though early improvements are partly due to neuromuscular adaptation rather than muscle growth. Visible changes in body composition take longer and depend heavily on diet. Managing expectations realistically helps maintain motivation during the period before results are visible."
      },
      {
        "id": "faq-wk-3",
        "question": "Can I do strength training every day?",
        "answer": "Working the same muscle groups every day doesn't allow adequate recovery and is more likely to cause injury or overtraining than to accelerate progress. Strength training the same muscles on consecutive days is generally not recommended. You can train on consecutive days if you alternate muscle groups — upper body one day, lower body the next, for example."
      },
      {
        "id": "faq-wk-4",
        "question": "What if I can't do 150 minutes a week?",
        "answer": "Any amount of physical activity is better than none. Research consistently shows that even small increases in movement — 60 to 90 minutes per week — produce measurable health benefits compared to being sedentary. Aim for what's realistic now and build gradually over time."
      }
    ],
